From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Justinas Grauslis <justinas@8devices.com>
Date: Tue, 16 Jun 2026 17:00:00 +0300
Subject: [PATCH] citron: support attribute-based A/B slot scheme

The Citron / robovision board does not use the Android A/B layout that
qbootctl was written for. It boots from the ESP (no boot_a/boot_b image), has
no androidboot 'slot_suffix' on the kernel cmdline, and only the dtb, efi and
system partition pairs carry the GPT slot-select attribute. eMMC is mmcblk1,
not mmcblk0. Out of the box qbootctl mis-detected the storage type, scanned
A/B pairs that are never provisioned here, could not tell which slot is
running, and aborted set-active with "Both A & B are inactive". This adapts it
to the board's simplified, attribute-only A/B scheme:

* Storage detection: treat any /dev/mmcblk* as eMMC, not only mmcblk0, so the
  eMMC GPT code path (not the UFS bsg path) is used.

* Managed partition set: restrict g_all_ptns to "dtb_a", "efi_a", "system_a" -
  the only pairs that carry slot attributes here - so set-active and the mark
  bootable/successful/unbootable ops don't walk (and abort on) unprovisioned
  pairs.

* Slot-reference base: replace the hard-coded "boot" base with a lookup that
  prefers "dtb" (dtb_a/dtb_b) and falls back to "efi" when the dtb pair is
  absent or carries no slot-select attribute; used by get_number_slots(),
  get_boot_attr() and (transitively) is_slot_marked_successful().

* Current slot: try slot_suffix first; when it is absent (this platform),
  derive the slot from the rootfs partition's GPT label on the kernel cmdline,
  e.g. root=PARTLABEL=system_a (or system_b). "current slot" stays sourced from
  the cmdline (the running slot) while "active slot" stays sourced from the GPT
  attribute, so the two can legitimately diverge if the bootloader fell back to
  the other slot. get_current_or_active_slot() yields -1 when the running slot
  cannot be determined from the cmdline (instead of guessing 0 / the active
  slot), and keeps -ENOENT for a device with no slots at all.

* Active boot slot: an all-zero active attribute is a valid uninitialised state
  (first boot, before any set-active), not an error. get_active_boot_slot() now
  returns int: the active slot index, -1 when no slot is marked active, or a
  negative error from the lower layer on a hard fault (no GPT / missing
  partition labels - still logged there). It no longer prints "Failed to find
  the active boot slot" nor defaults to slot 0 for the uninitialised case. The
  getActiveBootSlot HAL pointer is widened to int to carry this (getCurrentSlot
  is already int upstream).

* CLI output: every slot-reporting message prints a human slot name - "a"/"b"
  (the partition suffix without the leading underscore) or "none" when
  undetermined - via the slot_name() helper in qbootctl and the matching
  slot_letter() helper for the in-library mark_boot_successful / set-active
  messages. So the dump shows Active:0 on every slot and "-a"/"-c" print
  "Active slot: none" / "Current slot: none" rather than a bogus letter, and a
  running slot that is merely undetermined no longer trips the "No slots found"
  abort (only a true no-slots device, -ENOENT, still does). The raw "_a"/"_b"
  suffix is kept internal to partition naming.

* Drop the -x ("get the slot suffix") CLI option: remove it from the usage
  text and the getopt string so it is neither advertised nor parsed.

* set-active: when an attribute-only pair has both _a and _b clear, don't abort
  - assume the current slot (or slot 0 if the running slot is undetermined) and
  write the requested target. This is the bootstrap path that lets the GPT
  active attribute be provisioned the first time (upstream aborted, leaving
  set-active permanently unusable here); it does not change behaviour once a
  slot is already active. Also fixes the missing newline on the old message.

* set-active type GUID follows the active slot: route the "active" type GUID to
  the slot being activated (and the "inactive" GUID to the other), restoring the
  legacy C++ behaviour. The upstream C rewrite regressed this - it pinned
  active_guid to physical partition A and inactive_guid to B and let only the
  attribute flag track the target slot, so activating slot B left efi_b with the
  inactive (non-ESP) type GUID and the firmware could not boot it. With this the
  bootable/ESP type GUID always migrates onto the active slot's efi/dtb/system
  partition, exactly as the old tool (and the partition layout) assume.

* Demote "Couldn't find cmdline arg" to the debug log: it is expected here
  (no slot_suffix) and handled by the fallback, so it shouldn't spam normal
  output.

* Kernel cmdline parsing (get_kernel_cmdline_arg): honor the LAST occurrence of
  a repeated argument, matching the kernel, so root= resolves to the value the
  bootloader appends rather than the UKI's baked-in default (both are present
  here: "root=PARTLABEL=system_a ... root=PARTLABEL=system_<active>"). Also
  null-terminate the read buffer and stop the value copy at end-of-cmdline - the
  appended root= is the last token and has no trailing space, which the old
  first-match, space-only-terminated copy could not read correctly.

Ported to qbootctl 0.2.2 (upstream C rewrite; the original was against the
earlier C++ tree - .cpp files, vector<string> part lists and a bootctrl_test
stub that no longer exist).

Upstream-Status: Pending
Signed-off-by: Justinas Grauslis <justinas@8devices.com>
---
diff --git a/bootctrl.h b/bootctrl.h
index fcbd807..f72eb4f 100644
--- a/bootctrl.h
+++ b/bootctrl.h
@@ -109,8 +109,10 @@ struct boot_control_module {
 	* Returns the active slot to boot into on the next boot. If
 	* setActiveBootSlot() has been called, the getter function should return
 	* the same slot as the one provided in the last setActiveBootSlot() call.
+	* Returns -1 if no slot is marked active (a valid uninitialised state,
+	* e.g. first boot before any set-active), and -errno on hard error.
 	*/
-	unsigned (*getActiveBootSlot)();
+	int (*getActiveBootSlot)();
 };
 
 extern const struct boot_control_module bootctl;
diff --git a/bootctrl_impl.c b/bootctrl_impl.c
index 5586f72..f6b3773 100644
--- a/bootctrl_impl.c
+++ b/bootctrl_impl.c
@@ -94,25 +94,32 @@ void get_kernel_cmdline_arg(const char *arg, char *buf, const char *def)
 {
 	int fd;
 	char pcmd[MAX_CMDLINE_SIZE];
-	char *val, *found, *ptr = buf;
+	char *val, *found = NULL, *ptr = buf;
 	fd = open("/proc/cmdline", O_RDONLY);
-	int rc = read(fd, pcmd, MAX_CMDLINE_SIZE);
+	int rc = read(fd, pcmd, MAX_CMDLINE_SIZE - 1);
 	if (rc < 0) {
 		fprintf(stderr, "Couldn't open /proc/cmdline: %d (%s)\n", rc, strerror(errno));
 		goto error;
 	}
 	close(fd);
-	found = strstr(pcmd, arg);
+	pcmd[rc] = '\0';
+	// Kernel uses the LAST occurrence of a repeated arg (e.g. two root=: the
+	// UKI's baked default plus the one the bootloader appends), so take the
+	// last match, not the first.
+	for (char *p = pcmd; (p = strstr(p, arg)) != NULL; p++)
+		found = p;
 	if (!found || !(val = strstr(found, "="))) {
-		fprintf(stderr, "Couldn't find cmdline arg: '%s'\n", arg);
+		/* Not an error: callers fall back to the supplied default (and, for
+		 * slot_suffix, to root= parsing). Keep it debug-only to avoid noise. */
+		LOGD("Couldn't find cmdline arg: '%s'\n", arg);
 		goto error;
 	}
 
 	val++;
-	// no this doesn't handle quotes lol
-	while (*val != ' ') {
+	// Copy the value up to the next separator or the end of the cmdline.
+	while (*val && *val != ' ' && *val != '\n' && *val != '\t')
 		*ptr++ = *val++;
-	}
+	*ptr = '\0';
 
 	return;
 
@@ -262,6 +269,46 @@ static int update_slot_attribute(struct gpt_disk *disk, unsigned slot,
 	return 0;
 }
 
+/*
+ * Slot-reference partition base. This platform has no Android boot_a/_b, so
+ * prefer "dtb" (dtb_a/dtb_b) and fall back to "efi" (efi_a/efi_b) when the dtb
+ * pair is absent OR carries no slot-select attribute (the active bit is clear
+ * on both dtb_a and dtb_b, i.e. attr left at zero). Returns the first base
+ * whose active bit is set; otherwise the last existing pair; NULL only when
+ * neither pair exists.
+ */
+static const char *get_slot_ptn_base(void)
+{
+	static const char *const candidates[] = { "dtb", "efi", NULL };
+	const char *fallback = NULL;
+	struct gpt_disk disk = { 0 };
+	char path[PATH_MAX];
+	char part[MAX_GPT_NAME_SIZE + 1];
+	struct stat st;
+	unsigned i;
+
+	for (i = 0; candidates[i] != NULL; i++) {
+		snprintf(path, sizeof(path), "%s/%s%s", BOOTDEV_DIR,
+			 candidates[i], AB_SLOT_A_SUFFIX);
+		if (stat(path, &st) != 0)
+			continue;
+		/* Pair exists - remember it (last existing wins as fallback). */
+		fallback = candidates[i];
+		/* Prefer a base that actually encodes the active slot. */
+		snprintf(part, sizeof(part), "%s%s", candidates[i],
+			 AB_SLOT_A_SUFFIX);
+		if (get_partition_attribute(&disk, part, ATTR_SLOT_ACTIVE) == 1)
+			break;
+		snprintf(part, sizeof(part), "%s%s", candidates[i],
+			 AB_SLOT_B_SUFFIX);
+		if (get_partition_attribute(&disk, part, ATTR_SLOT_ACTIVE) == 1)
+			break;
+	}
+
+	gpt_disk_free(&disk);
+	return candidates[i] ? candidates[i] : fallback;
+}
+
 /*
  * Returns 0 for no slots, or the number of slots found.
  * Fun semantic note: Having "1" slot (ie just a "boot" partition)
@@ -274,6 +321,9 @@ unsigned get_number_slots()
 	struct dirent *de = NULL;
 	DIR *dir_bootdev = NULL;
 	static int slot_count = 0;
+	const char *base;
+	char ptn_prefix[MAX_GPT_NAME_SIZE];
+	char ptn_aging[MAX_GPT_NAME_SIZE];
 
 	// If we've already counted the slots, return the cached value.
 	// If there are no slots then we'll always rerun the search...
@@ -283,6 +333,14 @@ unsigned get_number_slots()
 	assert(AB_SLOT_A_SUFFIX[0] == '_');
 	assert(AB_SLOT_B_SUFFIX[0] == '_');
 
+	// No Android boot_a/_b here - count the pairs of the slot-reference base
+	// (dtb_*, or efi_* as fallback) instead.
+	base = get_slot_ptn_base();
+	if (!base)
+		return 0;
+	snprintf(ptn_prefix, sizeof(ptn_prefix), "%s_", base);
+	snprintf(ptn_aging, sizeof(ptn_aging), "%s_aging", base);
+
 	dir_bootdev = opendir(BOOTDEV_DIR);
 	// Shouldn't this be an assert?
 	if (!dir_bootdev) {
@@ -293,8 +351,8 @@ unsigned get_number_slots()
 	while ((de = readdir(dir_bootdev))) {
 		if (de->d_name[0] == '.')
 			continue;
-		if (!strncmp(de->d_name, BOOT_IMG_PTN_NAME, strlen(BOOT_IMG_PTN_NAME)) &&
-		    !!strncmp(de->d_name, "boot_aging\n", strlen("boot_aging"))) {
+		if (!strncmp(de->d_name, ptn_prefix, strlen(ptn_prefix)) &&
+		    !!strncmp(de->d_name, ptn_aging, strlen(ptn_aging))) {
 			slot_count++;
 		}
 	}
@@ -320,18 +378,23 @@ static int boot_control_check_slot_sanity(unsigned slot)
 int get_boot_attr(struct gpt_disk *disk, unsigned slot, enum part_attr_type attr)
 {
 	char bootPartition[MAX_GPT_NAME_SIZE + 1] = { 0 };
+	const char *base = get_slot_ptn_base();
 
 	if (boot_control_check_slot_sanity(slot) != 0) {
 		fprintf(stderr, "%s: Argument check failed\n", __func__);
 		return -1;
 	}
+	if (!base) {
+		fprintf(stderr, "%s: no dtb/efi A/B partitions found\n", __func__);
+		return -1;
+	}
 
-	snprintf(bootPartition, sizeof(bootPartition) - 1, "boot%s", slot_suffix_arr[slot]);
+	snprintf(bootPartition, sizeof(bootPartition) - 1, "%s%s", base, slot_suffix_arr[slot]);
 
 	return get_partition_attribute(disk, bootPartition, attr);
 }
 
-unsigned get_active_boot_slot()
+int get_active_boot_slot()
 {
 	struct gpt_disk disk = { 0 };
 	uint32_t num_slots = get_number_slots();
@@ -342,26 +405,43 @@ unsigned get_active_boot_slot()
 	}
 
 	for (uint32_t i = 0; i < num_slots; i++) {
-		if (get_boot_attr(&disk, i, ATTR_SLOT_ACTIVE)) {
+		int attr = get_boot_attr(&disk, i, ATTR_SLOT_ACTIVE);
+		if (attr < 0) {
+			// hard error (no GPT / partitions); already logged
+			gpt_disk_free(&disk);
+			return attr;
+		}
+		if (attr) {
 			gpt_disk_free(&disk);
 			return i;
 		}
 	}
 
-	fprintf(stderr, "%s: Failed to find the active boot slot\n", __func__);
 	gpt_disk_free(&disk);
-	return 0;
+	/*
+	 * No slot carries the active attribute. That is a valid, uninitialised
+	 * state (e.g. first boot, before any set-active) - not an error - so do
+	 * not log anything and report "no active slot" (-1) to the caller.
+	 */
+	return -1;
 }
 
 /*
- * The current slot is usually made available via the kernel cmdline. If it isn't for some reason
- * (e.g. because we booted via a secondary bootloader that removes Android cmdline args) then we
- * assume that the active slot is the current slot
+ * The current (running) slot. Sourced from the kernel cmdline, independently of
+ * the active slot (which comes from the GPT attribute), so the two can
+ * legitimately diverge if the bootloader fell back to the other slot.
+ *
+ * Preference order: androidboot 'slot_suffix'; then, on platforms without it,
+ * the rootfs partition's GPT label (root=PARTLABEL=system_a / _b). Returns -1
+ * when the running slot cannot be determined from the cmdline (rather than
+ * guessing slot 0), and -ENOENT when the device has no slots at all.
  */
 static int get_current_or_active_slot()
 {
 	uint32_t num_slots = 0;
 	char bootSlotProp[MAX_CMDLINE_SIZE] = { '\0' };
+	char rootArg[MAX_CMDLINE_SIZE] = { '\0' };
+	char want[32];
 	unsigned i = 0;
 	num_slots = get_number_slots();
 	if (num_slots == 0)
@@ -371,25 +451,26 @@ static int get_current_or_active_slot()
 		return 0;
 	}
 
-	get_kernel_cmdline_arg(BOOT_SLOT_PROP, bootSlotProp, "N/A");
-	if (!strncmp(bootSlotProp, "N/A\n", strlen("N/A"))) {
-		fprintf(stderr, "%s: Unable to read boot slot property\n", __func__);
-		return get_active_boot_slot();
+	// Preferred: androidboot-style 'slot_suffix' on the kernel cmdline.
+	get_kernel_cmdline_arg(BOOT_SLOT_PROP, bootSlotProp, "");
+	for (i = 0; slot_suffix_arr[i] != NULL; i++) {
+		if (bootSlotProp[0] &&
+		    !strncmp(bootSlotProp, slot_suffix_arr[i], strlen(slot_suffix_arr[i])))
+			return i;
 	}
 
-	// Iterate through a list of partitons named as boot+suffix
-	// and see which one is currently active.
+	// No slot_suffix on this platform - derive the current slot from the
+	// rootfs partition's GPT label, e.g. root=PARTLABEL=system_a (or _b).
+	get_kernel_cmdline_arg("root=", rootArg, "");
 	for (i = 0; slot_suffix_arr[i] != NULL; i++) {
-		if (!strncmp(bootSlotProp, slot_suffix_arr[i], strlen(slot_suffix_arr[i]))) {
-			// printf("%s current_slot = %d\n", __func__, i);
+		snprintf(want, sizeof(want), "PARTLABEL=system%s", slot_suffix_arr[i]);
+		if (strstr(rootArg, want))
 			return i;
-		}
 	}
 
-	// The HAL spec requires that we return a number between
-	// 0 to num_slots - 1. Since something went wrong here we
-	// are just going to return the default slot.
-	return 0;
+	// Nothing on the cmdline identifies the running slot - report it as
+	// undetermined (-1) rather than guessing the first slot.
+	return -1;
 }
 
 int is_slot_bootable(unsigned slot)
@@ -404,6 +485,16 @@ int is_slot_bootable(unsigned slot)
 	return -1;
 }
 
+// Slot letter ("a"/"b") for user-facing messages - the suffix without the
+// leading underscore. The full "_a"/"_b" (slot_suffix_arr) stays in use for
+// partition-name construction.
+static const char *slot_letter(unsigned slot)
+{
+	const char *s = slot_suffix_arr[slot];
+
+	return (s && s[0] == '_') ? s + 1 : s;
+}
+
 int mark_boot_successful(unsigned slot)
 {
 	struct gpt_disk disk = { 0 };
@@ -412,7 +503,7 @@ int mark_boot_successful(unsigned slot)
 	int ret = 0;
 
 	if (successful < 0 || unbootable < 0) {
-		fprintf(stderr, "SLOT %s: Failed to read attributes\n", slot_suffix_arr[slot]);
+		fprintf(stderr, "SLOT %s: Failed to read attributes\n", slot_letter(slot));
 		ret = -1;
 		goto out;
 	}
@@ -420,17 +511,17 @@ int mark_boot_successful(unsigned slot)
 	if (unbootable) {
 		printf("SLOT %s: was marked unbootable, fixing this"
 		       " (I hope you know what you're doing...)\n",
-		       slot_suffix_arr[slot]);
+		       slot_letter(slot));
 		update_slot_attribute(&disk, slot, ATTR_BOOTABLE);
 	}
 
 	if (successful) {
-		fprintf(stderr, "SLOT %s: already marked successful\n", slot_suffix_arr[slot]);
+		fprintf(stderr, "SLOT %s: already marked successful\n", slot_letter(slot));
 		goto out;
 	}
 
 	if (update_slot_attribute(&disk, slot, ATTR_BOOT_SUCCESSFUL)) {
-		fprintf(stderr, "SLOT %s: Failed to mark boot successful\n", slot_suffix_arr[slot]);
+		fprintf(stderr, "SLOT %s: Failed to mark boot successful\n", slot_letter(slot));
 		ret = -1;
 		goto out;
 	}
@@ -535,8 +626,29 @@ static int boot_ctl_set_active_slot_for_partitions(struct gpt_disk *disk,
 			memcpy((void *)active_guid, (const void *)pentryB, TYPE_GUID_SIZE);
 			memcpy((void *)inactive_guid, (const void *)pentryA, TYPE_GUID_SIZE);
 		} else {
-			fprintf(stderr, "Both A & B are inactive..Aborting");
-			return -1;
+			/*
+			 * Neither slot carries the active attribute. This board
+			 * selects the boot slot by GPT attribute and a freshly
+			 * provisioned (attribute-only) pair can legitimately have
+			 * both _a and _b clear, so do NOT abort - that left
+			 * set-active permanently broken. Assume the current slot
+			 * is the active one so the requested target can still be
+			 * written; if the running slot is undetermined, default to
+			 * slot 0 (_a).
+			 */
+			int cur = get_current_or_active_slot();
+			if (cur < 0)
+				cur = 0;
+			fprintf(stderr,
+				"%s: %s/%s both inactive, assuming current slot %s\n",
+				__func__, slotA, slotB, slot_letter(cur));
+			if (cur == 1) {
+				memcpy((void *)active_guid, (const void *)pentryB, TYPE_GUID_SIZE);
+				memcpy((void *)inactive_guid, (const void *)pentryA, TYPE_GUID_SIZE);
+			} else {
+				memcpy((void *)active_guid, (const void *)pentryA, TYPE_GUID_SIZE);
+				memcpy((void *)inactive_guid, (const void *)pentryB, TYPE_GUID_SIZE);
+			}
 		}
 		int a_state = slot == 0 ? SLOT_ACTIVE : SLOT_INACTIVE;
 		int b_state = slot == 1 ? SLOT_ACTIVE : SLOT_INACTIVE;
@@ -548,14 +660,26 @@ static int boot_ctl_set_active_slot_for_partitions(struct gpt_disk *disk,
 			return -1;
 		}
 
-		// Mark A as active in primary table
-		UPDATE_SLOT(pentryA, active_guid, a_state);
-		// Mark A as active in backup table
-		UPDATE_SLOT(pentryA_bak, active_guid, a_state);
-		// Mark B as inactive in primary table
-		UPDATE_SLOT(pentryB, inactive_guid, b_state);
-		// Mark B as inactive in backup table
-		UPDATE_SLOT(pentryB_bak, inactive_guid, b_state);
+		/*
+		 * Route the "active" type GUID to the slot being activated (and the
+		 * "inactive" GUID to the other slot), mirroring the legacy C++ tool.
+		 * The bootable/ESP type GUID must live on the ACTIVE slot; upstream
+		 * pinned active_guid to physical partition A and moved only the
+		 * attribute flag, which left the activated slot with the wrong GUID.
+		 */
+		if (slot == 0) {
+			// Slot A active: A takes the active GUID, B the inactive one.
+			UPDATE_SLOT(pentryA, active_guid, a_state);
+			UPDATE_SLOT(pentryA_bak, active_guid, a_state);
+			UPDATE_SLOT(pentryB, inactive_guid, b_state);
+			UPDATE_SLOT(pentryB_bak, inactive_guid, b_state);
+		} else {
+			// Slot B active: B takes the active GUID, A the inactive one.
+			UPDATE_SLOT(pentryB, active_guid, b_state);
+			UPDATE_SLOT(pentryB_bak, active_guid, b_state);
+			UPDATE_SLOT(pentryA, inactive_guid, a_state);
+			UPDATE_SLOT(pentryA_bak, inactive_guid, a_state);
+		}
 	}
 
 	// write updated content to disk
diff --git a/gpt-utils.c b/gpt-utils.c
index fe678a3..ffcd8a2 100644
--- a/gpt-utils.c
+++ b/gpt-utils.c
@@ -802,9 +802,9 @@ error:
 // Determine whether to handle the given partition as eMMC or UFS, using the
 // name of the backing device.
 //
-// Note: In undefined cases (i.e. /dev/mmcblk1 and unresolvable), this function
-// will tend to prefer UFS behavior. If it incorrectly reports this, then the
-// program should exit (e.g. by failing) before making any changes.
+// Note: matches any /dev/mmcblk* device as eMMC; UFS resolves to /dev/sd*.
+// In unresolvable cases this returns false (UFS behavior) and the caller
+// should fail before making changes.
 bool gpt_utils_is_partition_backed_by_emmc(const char *part)
 {
 	char devpath[GPT_PTN_PATH_MAX] = { '\0' };
@@ -812,5 +812,7 @@ bool gpt_utils_is_partition_backed_by_emmc(const char *part)
 	if (get_dev_path_from_partition_name(part, devpath, sizeof(devpath)))
 		return false;
 
-	return !strcmp(devpath, EMMC_DEVICE);
+	/* Any eMMC block device (mmcblk0, mmcblk1, ...) - not just mmcblk0 -
+	 * is eMMC-backed. UFS partitions resolve to /dev/sd* instead. */
+	return !strncmp(devpath, "/dev/mmcblk", strlen("/dev/mmcblk"));
 }
diff --git a/gpt-utils.h b/gpt-utils.h
index b496d70..0d4fdfc 100644
--- a/gpt-utils.h
+++ b/gpt-utils.h
@@ -82,9 +82,15 @@ extern "C" {
 	"hyp_a", "keymaster_a", "msadp_a", "qupfw_a", "storsec_a", "tz_a", \
 	"vbmeta_a", "vbmeta_system_a"
 
+/*
+ * 8devices: on this platform only dtb, efi and system carry the GPT slot-select
+ * attributes. Restrict the managed A/B partition set to those three, so
+ * set-active and the mark bootable/successful/unbootable ops do not walk - and
+ * abort on ("Both A & B are inactive") - the other A/B pairs whose slot
+ * attributes are never provisioned here.
+ */
 static const char g_all_ptns[][MAX_GPT_NAME_SIZE + 1] = {
-	PTN_SWAP_LIST, "boot_a", "system_a",
-	"vendor_a", "modem_a", "system_ext_a", "product_a"
+	"dtb_a", "efi_a", "system_a"
 };
 
 // No more than /dev/sdk
diff --git a/qbootctl.c b/qbootctl.c
index a369fbc..5de6c6d 100644
--- a/qbootctl.c
+++ b/qbootctl.c
@@ -77,14 +77,13 @@ int usage()
 	// clang-format off
